 true] [17592186406683 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406671 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406683 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406679 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406683 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406683 1077 "selection" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406683 1079 #edb/ref 17592186406667 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406683 1080 #uuid "90932faf-686a-5262-b048-f69de0f0ccfb" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406684 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406672 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406684 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406674 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406684 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406676 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406684 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406678 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406684 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406681 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406684 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406684 1077 "selection" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406684 1079 #edb/ref 17592186406672 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406684 1080 #uuid "ac075b6f-3299-5310-ad52-26f9cf648385" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406685 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406682 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406685 1069 "q-40514" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406685 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406685 1072 #uuid "8a69da2c-f673-5e64-a265-9fe904a3c9ad" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406685 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406683 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406686 1069 "q-40491" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406686 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\n- $U={Q([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])}$\n- $W={Q([\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])}$\n- $V={Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])}$" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406686 1072 #uuid "9892c6c7-24de-57f2-a3e8-0b32de0c1349" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406686 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})},\\,$ ${Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})},\\,$ and $\\,{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})}.$ \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=18-18=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=1-1=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"." 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406684 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406687 1069 "q-40488" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406687 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406687 1072 #uuid "cf616da5-d6d0-5837-a9fb-64c2cf137040" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406687 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406688 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406689 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406688 1062 #uuid "0bda9774-6623-5015-9c4f-2876bc204742" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406688 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406687 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406689 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406690 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406689 1062 #uuid "5c0620be-2d88-50a9-b295-5b90712b0a8b" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406689 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406686 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406690 1062 #uuid "c999b8fd-0d25-5711-adf5-8e3f0c95ef68" 13194139533512 17592186045585 true] [17592186406690 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406685 13194139533512 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"activity-11d38e3f-6339-5429-8d89-8272fca9a35c" 17592186406666 "answer-0dfd22c3-475e-57e7-a43b-2426876d96a9" 17592186406667 "answer-18261590-5d63-59fa-a5a2-24d4904d43be" 17592186406668 "answer-24e42699-a0f0-5e81-a194-c91514b7e8ae" 17592186406669 "answer-25feb7c8-dd5c-59a1-bec8-c633a782af5e" 17592186406670 "answer-2eca64ea-f21c-59ab-b00b-5d3ef14bd0fb" 17592186406671 "answer-33d47db5-c434-55d6-9456-167ab8a0b67c" 17592186406672 "answer-46c27bd0-b639-51d1-a04d-8098da45991e" 17592186406673 "answer-7c3a74ea-6a6d-5da3-b53b-157fcb966ead" 17592186406674 "answer-b3565871-fc75-5a3f-bb6e-506cbb0458f5" 17592186406675 "answer-b81c5bc9-bfe5-5ce5-928d-fa684e207472" 17592186406676 "answer-bfd57b0f-3965-5b70-833f-7fa36e06ae4f" 17592186406677 "answer-c6a8591a-2779-5016-a0d3-d51746099b13" 17592186406678 "answer-d2e6fe6e-e075-5371-baff-975eb86d75ba" 17592186406679 "answer-e14e0ad1-a7b2-5023-b79c-bff39fbd35df" 17592186406680 "answer-f3f62bc1-8a13-5efa-ad